// NEGATIVE TEST -- `macro_rules!` is refused BY NAME (N3-14).
//
// The requirement is not "macro_rules! is unimplemented". It is that there is
// no declarative/procedural SPLIT: this language has one macro system, so a
// second one is refused because the first exists rather than because nothing
// does.
//
// WHAT THE DIAGNOSTIC USED TO BE: `macro_rules!` fell through the item parser
// to "Expected function, struct, enum, trait, type, impl, or macro declaration"
// -- seven nouns, one of which is `macro`, which a reader arriving from Rust
// reads as "macros are not supported here". Exactly backwards.
//
// The same refusal answers the invocation spelling `macro_rules!(...)`, which
// used to be "Unknown macro 'macro_rules'" -- a sentence that reads as a typo
// in the NAME when the problem is the SYSTEM (src/macros/mod.rs, `unknown_macro`).
macro_rules! square {
    ($x:expr) => {
        $x * $x
    };
}

fn main() {
    print_int(square!(4));
}
